story=[]
def P(s,style='Explain'):story.append(Paragraph(s,styles[style]))
def E(i):
 im=PILImage.open(BASE/'equations'/f'{i}.png');w,h=im.size;scale=min(480/w,1/2.1);story.append(Image(str(BASE/'equations'/f'{i}.png'),width=w*scale,height=h*scale));story.append(Spacer(1,8))
def footer(c,d):
 c.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor('#CBD6DF'));c.line(54,44,558,44);c.setFont('Helvetica',8);c.setFillColor(colors.HexColor('#526A80'));c.drawString(54,30,'Alaska 2026 | Mathematical methods | November 17, 2026');c.drawRightString(558,30,str(d.page))
P('From ballot rankings to a candidate axis','DocTitle')
P('A mathematical companion to the Alaska ranked-ballot presentation. These equations describe the method implemented in our notebook.','SmallNote')
P('MDS (multidimensional scaling) summarizes candidate relationships on a line. The input is the valid named ranking sequence in each race, derived from Rachel/FairVote\'s November 11 CSV snapshot. It is not a direct measurement of ideology.')
P('1. Represent the valid ballot rankings','Step');E(0)
P('Here b indexes distinct rankings, w<sub>b</sub> is their ballot count, and the sequence has length l<sub>b</sub>. We apply Alaska\'s overvote, skipped-rank and repeated-name rules before omitting generic write-ins from the named sequence. Original rank cells remain preserved. Unranked candidates receive no invented preferences.')
P('2. Count candidate-pair relationships','Step');E(1);E(2)
P('C<sub>ij</sub> counts ballots ranking both candidates. A<sub>ij</sub> counts ballots where j immediately follows i in the valid named sequence. The indicator <b>1</b>{condition} equals 1 when the condition holds and 0 otherwise. We weight each ranking by its actual ballot count.')
P('“Adjacent” refers to the interpreted named sequence, not necessarily neighboring original rank cells. A permitted skipped rank can be crossed, and generic write-ins are omitted only after applying ranking barriers.','SmallNote')
story.append(PageBreak())
P('Dissimilarities and the MDS fit','DocTitle')
P('3. Convert adjacency into dissimilarity','Step');E(3);E(4)
P('Set F<sub>ij</sub> to zero if C<sub>ij</sub> is zero. S<sub>ij</sub> combines both adjacency directions. More frequent adjacency gives larger similarity and smaller dissimilarity. This is an adjacency-based association measure, not a measure of agreement on policy.')
P('Zero-similarity convention: for an off-diagonal pair with S<sub>ij</sub> = 0, our implementation substitutes 2 / S<sub>min</sub><super>+</super>, where S<sub>min</sub><super>+</super> is the smallest positive similarity. If no pair has positive similarity, the axis cannot be estimated. This finite substitution is an implementation convention, not an observed distance.','SmallNote')
P('4. Fit candidates onto one line','Step');E(5);E(6)
P('x<sub>i</sub> is candidate i\'s fitted position. Nonmetric MDS numerically searches for positions and an order-preserving transformation f that make stress small. Larger dissimilarities must correspond to no smaller transformed dissimilarities. The solver normalizes scale to avoid a trivial collapsed solution.')
P('The algorithm alternates monotonic regression and stress-reducing coordinate updates. It aims to reproduce the ordering of dissimilarities; it does not require fitted distances to equal the original dissimilarities. Repeated starts help address local solutions but do not guarantee a global optimum.')
P('The equation above describes the fitting mismatch. The notebook requests normalized stress for its diagnostic output. Stress measures fit to pair dissimilarities; gamma, described on page 4, measures compatibility of ballot rankings. They are different quantities.','SmallNote')
story.append(PageBreak())
P('Repeated fits and the displayed axis','DocTitle')
P('5. Repeat, align and average','Step');E(7);E(8)
P('Run one-dimensional nonmetric MDS repeatedly with different random starts. Center each configuration and align mirrored solutions, because reversing a line leaves all pairwise distances unchanged. Configurations with coincident candidate positions are discarded under the implementation\'s numerical tolerance.')
P('Select the most frequently recovered candidate ordering. R* denotes the aligned runs with that order. Average their centered coordinates. Ties between modal orders are broken by mean stress and then deterministically by candidate order. We do not average across incompatible candidate orders.')
P('The saved observed fits use 20 outer runs with 8 initializations per run. A stronger fit-budget sensitivity analysis preserves the candidate order and gamma; it is not used to silently replace the saved main result.','SmallNote')
P('6. Normalize the axis','Step');E(9)
P('The minimum averaged coordinate becomes 0 and the maximum becomes 1. A display reflection z\' = 1 - z changes orientation without changing distances. Each race is fitted and normalized separately; equal numerical coordinates across races are not a common calibrated ideology measurement.')
P('Senate result: Peltola 0.000; Dan J. 0.436; Heikes 0.751; Daniel S. 1.000. Dan J. denotes the outsider (source: Sullivan, Daniel J. Jr.); Daniel S. denotes the incumbent (source: Sullivan, Dan S.).')
story.append(PageBreak())
P('What happens after fitting the axis?','DocTitle')
P('Ballot consistency (gamma)','Step');E(10);E(11)
P('For a stated group of nonempty valid named rankings, a ranking is compatible if some voter position u on the fitted line makes the recorded candidates strictly increasing in distance. Candidates not ranked on that ballot impose no additional comparison constraints. The numerator counts compatible ballots; the denominator is the total number of ballots in that stated scoring group.')
P('One- and two-name rankings automatically pass this compatibility criterion. The main comparison therefore highlights three- and four-name rankings. Senate gamma for that group is 61.54%, compared with a 41.57% mean across 49 randomized runs preserving first choice and ranking length; each random dataset receives its own independently fitted axis.')
P('Estimated voter positions','Step')
P('Candidate positions are the fitted MDS points. Estimated voter positions are subsequently allocated across the locations compatible with each ranking, under Mahshad\'s convention. Single-name rankings are placed at their candidate. These allocations are not uniquely observed voter locations. Spatially inconsistent rankings can still contain valid Alaska rankings and remain part of the source data.')
P('Method and references','Step')
P('Project implementation: Alaska_2026_Statewide_Analysis.ipynb; analysis/shared/og_axis.py (pair_matrices, dissimilarity, fit_axis); analysis/shared/fit_races.py. Ballot inputs: Rachel/FairVote\'s unmodified November 11, 2026 Senate, House and governor CSVs.','SmallNote')
P('<link href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/manifold.html#multidimensional-scaling" color="#347CB1">Scikit-learn: multidimensional scaling and nonmetric stress</link><br/><link href="https://www.elections.alaska.gov/election-information/" color="#347CB1">Alaska Division of Elections: ranking rules</link>','SmallNote')
P('Scope: the candidate axes describe ranking structure in a dated snapshot. They do not establish political motivation, voter confusion, official final election outcomes, or a calibrated ideology scale.','SmallNote')
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(str(BASE/'Alaska_MDS_Mathematical_Methods.pdf'),pagesize=(612,792),rightMargin=54,leftMargin=54,topMargin=48,bottomMargin=60,title='Alaska MDS: Mathematical Methods',author='Pejmon Shariati')
